[turn] round 1 AG1 defeat (rebut on Conc)
  id: arg-fce1f579bc
  target: arg-09ef9bd8e0
  target_statement: For low-income families who depend on predictable school-day coverage for childcare/attendance, that means the “school” side of the scheduling constraint can remain even when Election Day is designated as a federal holiday.
Even for low-income families that rely on predictable school-day childcare/attendance, making Election Day a national holiday primarily reduces the constraint that forces parents into fixed work schedules. When employers treat the day as a holiday (closing or operating on reduced hours), parents and other voters gain workable, daytime time to vote. In that situation, school-day coverage does not function as a continuing binding barrier in the way the target suggests, because voters are no longer simultaneously constrained to be at work at the same hours they would otherwise be voting. Thus, school attendance may still be relevant for some families, but it does not follow that the “school” side of the scheduling constraint will remain determinative when work schedules are loosened by a holiday.

